    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p23-q3-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Find the probability that a randomly selected accident in the study is classed as ‘major’.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$0.27$",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}P(\\text{Major})&=\\frac{54}{200}\\\\P(\\text{Major})&=0.27\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 20
      },
      {
        "id": "p23-q3-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Find the probability that an accident was classed as major given that it collided at a speed greater than 10 km/h over the local speed limit.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\frac{21}{38}$",
          "worked": "Of the 38 accidents involving a speed greater than 10 km/h over the limit, 21 were major.\n\n$$P(\\text{Major}\\mid\\text{more than 10 km/h over limit})=\\frac{21}{38}.$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 20
      },
      {
        "id": "p23-q3-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Hence, explain why accidents in this study classed as ‘major’ are not independent of the event that they were travelling at a speed greater than 10 km/h over the limit.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "The events are not independent because $P(\\text{Major}\\mid\\text{more than 10 }\\text{km/h}\\text{ over limit})=\\frac{21}{38}\\ne0.27=P(\\text{Major})$.",
          "worked": "From parts a and b, $P(\\text{Major})=0.27$ and $P(\\text{Major}\\mid\\text{more than 10 }\\text{km/h}\\text{ over limit})=\\frac{21}{38}$. These probabilities differ, so knowing that the vehicle was travelling more than 10 km/h over the limit changes the probability of a major accident. Therefore, the events are not independent.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 25
      },
      {
        "id": "p23-q3-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "By focussing on the events, ‘Minor’ accident and ‘Not speeding’, conduct an alternative test to that used in part c to show that they are also not independent events.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$P(\\text{Minor}\\cap\\text{Not speeding})=\\frac{41}{200}\\ne\\frac{146}{200}\\times\\frac{43}{200}$. Therefore, the events are not independent.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}P(\\text{Minor}\\cap\\text{Not speeding})&=\\frac{41}{200}\\\\P(\\text{Minor}\\cap\\text{Not speeding})&=0.205\\\\P(\\text{Minor})\\times P(\\text{Not speeding})&=\\frac{146}{200}\\times\\frac{43}{200}\\\\P(\\text{Minor})\\times P(\\text{Not speeding})&=0.15695\\end{align*}$$\n\nSince $0.205\\ne0.15695$, the intersection probability is not the product of the individual probabilities. Therefore, the events are not independent.",
